    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d289">

                <pb facs="0269" n="257" xml:id="pg257"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">891.24/259: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Iran</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Dreyfus</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Tehran</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-10-06T16:00:00+03:25">October 6, 1942—4
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received October 6—11:25 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>312. Department’s 257, October 2. After sending my No. 289, September 14, I had
                    several conversations with Prime Minister and Cabinet members concerning
                    Millspaugh in which is [<hi rendition="#italic">I?</hi>] stressed advantages of
                    employing a person not only highly qualified but also familiar with Iran.
                    Yesterday Foreign Minister<anchor n="30" xml:id="d289-f1"/> called on me to say
                    that Cabinet on Saturday formally approved employment of Millspaugh on
                    understanding that he will be supported by capable assistants. I explained that
                    this angle has already been given consideration by Department. Foreign Minister
                    will telegraph Iran Minister, Washington of Millspaugh’s approval.<anchor n="31"
                        xml:id="d289-f2"/> Foreign Minister told me in confidence Millspaugh’s
                    appointment had been opposed by Bader<anchor n="32" xml:id="d289-f3"/> and
                    Kazemi.</p>
                <p>On Sunday Finance Minister withdrew a bill earlier presented to Majlis for
                    appointment of a Swiss bank expert for Banque Mellie. This probably was result
                    of my remarks to Cabinet members that a Swiss banking expert seemed unnecessary
                    in view of contemplated employment of American Financial Mission.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dreyfus</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d289-f1" n="30" xml:id="d289-f1-content">
                <p>Ali Soheily.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d289-f2" n="31" xml:id="d289-f2-content">
                <p>On October 7 the Iranian Minister informed the Department that he had been
                    instructed to engage Dr. Millspaugh on the understanding that he would be
                    assisted by a competent group of experts satisfactory to the Department and to
                    the Iranian Government (891.51 A/531).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d289-f3" n="32" xml:id="d289-f3-content">
                <p>Probably Mahmoud Bader, former Minister of Finance.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
